        directAnswer: "OKX's platform collateralized borrowing limit is a risk-control checklist, not a signal that a user personally hit a balance limit. OKX says the platform-wide limit for each collateral cryptocurrency considers liquidity, trading depth, total platform volume and borrowing volume. It also says the effect varies by account mode and borrowing state: some spot-mode activities are unaffected, while borrowing-enabled modes require users to understand adjusted equity, collateral status and borrowing restrictions. OKX's margin introduction separately says maximum borrowing depends on user-level borrowing limits, tiered position limits and flexible Simple Earn pool limits. The user outcome is to record account mode, borrowing-on status, collateral asset, adjusted-equity treatment and the smallest non-value-moving API or UI check before assuming a failed borrow or transfer is a platform outage.",
