        llmsExcerpt: "Coinbase and Gemini both push users toward stronger authentication and destination controls, but they package the risk differently. Coinbase says 2-step verification is required, recommends multiple backup methods, supports passkeys and security keys, and uses address-book allowlisting to restrict sends to saved addresses after a 48-hour availability period. Coinbase Exchange documentation separately says whitelisting only allows withdrawals to addresses already in the Address Book and that API users cannot enable or disable whitelisting through the API. Gemini says 2FA is required and cannot be disabled, passkeys become the default 2FA method after opt-in, and approved-address or Withdrawal Protection flows can require a 7-day waiting period or multi-user approval before a wallet address can be used. The useful comparison is not which brand is safer in the abstract; it is whether the user's recovery method, address wait, API boundary and approval role are documented before funds move."
