        directAnswer: "A safe Bitstamp security setup starts with authentication, then narrows withdrawal destinations, and only then considers API or Travel Rule edge cases. Bitstamp by Robinhood's security guide says users should protect both the account and registered email with 2FA, and says Bitstamp security codes are required when confirming crypto withdrawals. The same guide says whitelisting lets users specify trusted addresses and makes those addresses the only destinations to which withdrawals can be made. Bitstamp's product announcement says withdrawal address whitelisting requires any address used for crypto withdrawal to be added to the whitelist before withdrawal. Bitstamp's API changelog adds a current compliance edge: crypto withdrawals subject to Travel Rule may require address verification, and API errors can state that the address provided is not verified. The tutorial outcome is a non-secret checklist: official domain, 2FA app, email control, whitelist status, address verification and stop conditions.",
