        llmsExcerpt: "Active-session and device-revocation pages are a practical security signal because they show whether an exchange explains how to notice and remove unwanted access. Coinbase says users can see active sessions, authorized mobile applications, web sessions and confirmed devices, and remove unauthorized access. Kraken says its Security section includes session and device management and lets users end individual or all sessions. Binance Academy says users can view authorized devices through Device Management and remove unrecognized devices. The radar takeaway is narrow: these pages do not prove a user's account is safe, but they define the evidence a user should collect before changing passwords, resetting 2FA or moving assets. Before declaring an exchange account clean after a suspicious login, check the official active-session or device-management page, record non-secret session labels, remove unknown devices, rotate credentials and verify withdrawal controls. Stop if the platform cannot show recent activity, if a confirmation email is unexpected, if a session cannot be revoked, or if the next evidence would expose passwords, codes, identity documents or balances."
