        llmsExcerpt: "A Bitstamp onboarding workflow should be treated as account security and verification, not as a reward claim. Bitstamp's Learn materials describe KYC as the process financial companies use to identify clients and assess risk, describe 2FA as a second authentication factor, and describe withdrawal whitelisting as a way to restrict withdrawals to trusted addresses. Bitstamp's API documentation also shows that crypto withdrawals can fail when a user is not verified, when an address is not verified, or when a key is bound to another withdrawal address. The safe tutorial is to verify the official site, secure login and email, complete identity checks only in the account area, and add withdrawal destinations deliberately. Before funding a new Bitstamp account, confirm the official domain, use a unique password and 2FA, complete KYC only inside the signed-in account flow, secure the registered email, enable withdrawal controls before large balances arrive, send only a small first transfer, and stop if any message asks for credentials, wallet private keys, seed phrases, remote-access software or a payment to unlock verification."
