        directAnswer: "Swift says its blockchain-based ledger is ready for initial use and that 17 banks from six continents are preparing to pilot live transactions using tokenised deposits for 24/7 cross-border payments. The ledger is described as an orchestration layer for bank-issued tokenised deposits before final settlement through existing systems. The operational question is whether a bank or corporate treasury can map membership, ledger messages, compliance checks, liquidity timing and fallback settlement before treating the pilot as production cash mobility. Treat Swift tokenised deposits as regulated-infrastructure readiness evidence, not a retail crypto payment promise. Before relying on it, map participating bank scope, customer eligibility, tokenised-deposit legal claim, orchestration-versus-final-settlement boundary, weekend liquidity process, compliance review, reconciliation and what happens if a ledger leg or legacy settlement leg fails.",
