        directAnswer: "Permissioned and permissionless tokenization architectures solve different coordination problems and neither label proves safety or scale. The BIS 2026 assessment says permissionless systems can broaden access but often face fragmented liquidity, governance and operational dependencies, while permissioned systems can embed identity and compliance but may become closed silos. For regulated settlement, the decisive questions are the legal asset, participant rules, validator governance, settlement asset, finality, privacy, interoperability and recovery path. A tokenized interface without a credible settlement anchor and operating rulebook is not a complete market infrastructure.",
